---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StatusBar from "../components/StatusBar.astro";
import Screenshot from "../components/Screenshot.astro";
import ShotRow from "../components/ShotRow.astro";
import Tip from "../components/Tip.astro";
const description =
  "mishmesh manual - getting around, messaging, contacts, repeaters, clock and settings, with the hard-to-find gestures explained in context.";
---
<Base title="mishmesh manual" description={description} bodyClass="manual" css="manual.css">
  <StatusBar variant="manual" />
<div class="doc">
  <aside class="side">
    <p class="lbl">Manual</p>
    <nav id="toc">
      <a href="#getting-around" class="on">Getting around</a>
      <a href="#messaging">Messaging</a>
      <a href="#contacts">Contacts</a>
      <a href="#repeaters">Repeaters &amp; rooms</a>
      <a href="#clock">Clock &amp; Airtime</a>
      <a href="#settings">Settings</a>
    </nav>
  </aside>

  <article>
    <!-- GETTING AROUND -->
    <section class="sec" id="getting-around">
      <span class="head">Getting around</span>
      <p>mishmesh is a stack of screens driven entirely from the device's buttons. <b>Select</b> drills into whatever's highlighted; <b>Back</b> steps out. Left, right, up and down move the highlight. That's the whole grammar - here's the shell you'll live in.</p>

      <h3>The home screen</h3>
      <p>The clock is home base. The top bar shows your node name and battery, with small status icons that appear only when they matter: muted, GPS searching or fixed, Bluetooth on or connected, repeater mode, an armed alarm, a running timer or stopwatch. Under the clock sits your unread-message count.</p>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/home.png" alt="home screen" caption="The hint bar along the bottom is your launchpad." />
      <p>That bottom hint bar - <code class="inl">&lt;Cont  .Apps  vTgls  Mess&gt;</code> - is how you leave home. <b>Select</b> opens the app menu. <b>Down</b> pulls the toggle drawer. The two side arrows are shortcuts you assign.</p>
      <Tip label="Shortcut"><p>The Left and Right labels on the hint bar aren't fixed. Settings &gt; Home &gt; Left / Right shortcut points each arrow at any app, so your two most-used screens are one press away.</p></Tip>

      <h3>The app menu</h3>
      <p>Select from home to open the grid of every screen. The Messages tile carries a badge when you have unread mail.</p>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/appmenu.png" alt="app menu grid" caption="Every screen, one grid." />

      <h3>The toggle drawer</h3>
      <p>Press <b>Down</b> from home to pull down a shade of quick toggles: Bluetooth, Sound (cycles the volume), GPS, Advert, and Theme (dark / light).</p>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/drawer.png" alt="quick-toggle drawer" caption="The toggle drawer, on the Sound tile." />
      <Tip label="Hidden"><p>On the <b>Advert</b> tile a short press sends a zero-hop advert; <b>hold</b> it to flood the advert across the whole mesh.</p><p>The Bluetooth tile shows your pairing PIN inline, and GPS shows the live satellite count while it searches.</p></Tip>

      <h3>Locking the screen</h3>
      <Tip label="Gesture"><p>From the home screen, press <kbd>Back</kbd> <kbd>Back</kbd> <kbd>Back</kbd> quickly to lock - an animated padlock appears. Three more Backs unlock it. Back does nothing else at home, so it's a safe pocket gesture.</p></Tip>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/lock.png" alt="locked screen with padlock" caption="Locked." />

      <h3>Typing</h3>
      <p>Text entry is a Nokia-style multi-tap keypad. Tap a key to cycle its letters; pause and it commits. A one-shot Shift gives you a single capital.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The bottom-left key cycles the whole pad between <b>letters, symbols and emoji</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Long-press</b> a letter key to type its digit instead.</li>
        <li><b>Up</b> from the top row focuses the language button; Select opens a layout picker for non-English keyboards.</li>
      </ul>
      <ShotRow>
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/keypad.png" alt="keypad letters" caption="Multi-tap letters." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/keypad_emoji.png" alt="keypad emoji page" caption="The emoji page." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/keypad_lang.png" alt="keypad language picker" caption="Keyboard languages." />
      </ShotRow>
    </section>

    <!-- MESSAGING -->
    <section class="sec" id="messaging">
      <span class="head">Messaging</span>
      <p>The Messages app has three tabs - <b>Chats</b>, <b>New</b>, and <b>Settings</b>. Chats lists your conversations with unread counts; open one to read and reply.</p>

      <h3>Starting something</h3>
      <p>The New tab covers every way in: a new direct message (pick a contact), create a private channel, join a private channel by name and hex key, join a public channel, or join a hashtag channel.</p>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/messages_new.png" alt="messages New tab" caption="The New tab." />

      <h3>In a thread</h3>
      <p>Your messages are boxed; incoming ones are labelled with the sender. Two compose buttons sit at the bottom: <b>Write</b> opens the keypad, <b>Quick</b> inserts a canned reply. Long-press any message for Reply, Resend, Delete, and View path.</p>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/chat.png" alt="chat thread with delivery status" caption="Delivery status sits under each sent message." />
      <p>Under each message you send you'll see its state: <b>Sent</b>, <b>Retrying N/5</b>, <b>Delivered</b> with the round-trip time, <b>Failed</b>, or <b>Heard N</b> for channel messages that were repeated.</p>
      <Tip label="Auto-retry"><p>Direct messages retry on their own - up to five times, every eight seconds. On the second retry mishmesh can drop the learned route and flood instead, so a stale path repairs itself. Both behaviours are toggles in Messages &gt; Settings (Auto retry DMs, Auto reset DM paths). Retrying only covers messages sent since the radio last started - a message still undelivered when you reboot is shown as Failed rather than picked up again.</p></Tip>

      <h3>Sharing a channel</h3>
      <p>Long-press a channel chat and choose Share to render it as a QR code (a <code class="inl">meshcore:channel/add</code> link). Another device scans it to join. Select toggles between the QR and the raw hex key.</p>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/channel_share.png" alt="channel share QR code" caption="A channel as a scannable QR." />
      <Tip label="Region"><p>Every chat can carry a free-text "region" tag - long-press the chat and edit region. It's a local label to group conversations however suits you; clearing it sets it back to None.</p></Tip>

      <h3>Where did it go?</h3>
      <p>View path on a message traces its hops and resolves each one to a contact name where it can. For channel messages it lists who repeated it, with hop count and signal-to-noise.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- CONTACTS -->
    <section class="sec" id="contacts">
      <span class="head">Contacts</span>
      <p>Contacts are grouped into tabs - <b>Favorites</b>, <b>Contacts</b>, <b>Repeaters</b>, <b>Rooms</b>, <b>Sensors</b>, and <b>Discover</b> (nodes you've heard but haven't added). Favorites only appears once you have some.</p>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/contact_detail.png" alt="contact detail card and actions" caption="A contact card and its actions." />
      <p>Open a contact for its card. The actions depend on what it is:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Send message</b> - users and rooms.</li>
        <li><b>Manage</b> - repeaters (see the next section).</li>
        <li><b>View details</b> - type, path (flood or direct), distance from your GPS, last-heard age, coordinates, and the full 32-byte public key.</li>
        <li><b>Telemetry</b> - pull live battery and sensor readings; a dialog waits for the reply.</li>
        <li><b>Ping</b> - a zero-hop round trip to a repeater, reporting time and signal.</li>
        <li><b>Set path / Reset path</b> - pin a manual route in hex, or clear the learned one back to flood.</li>
        <li><b>Rename</b>, <b>favorite</b>, <b>clear conversation</b>, <b>delete</b>.</li>
      </ul>
      <Tip label="Permissions"><p>Telemetry cuts both ways. A contact's Permissions screen controls what <b>they</b> can pull from <b>you</b>: whether to allow telemetry at all, and whether to include your location and sensors.</p></Tip>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/permissions.png" alt="contact telemetry permissions" caption="What a contact may pull from you." />
    </section>

    <!-- REPEATERS -->
    <section class="sec" id="repeaters">
      <span class="head">Repeaters &amp; rooms</span>
      <p>mishmesh administers a repeater over the air - no cable, no laptop. Open a repeater contact, choose <b>Manage</b>, and log in with its password. The login lasts until the device power-cycles. Room servers work the same way: you log in before you can post.</p>
      <ShotRow>
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/repeater_login.png" alt="repeater password entry" caption="Log in with the repeater password." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/repeater.png" alt="repeater live status" caption="Live health, fetched over the mesh." />
      </ShotRow>
      <p>A logged-in repeater has three tabs:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Status</b> - live health: uptime, battery, air time in and out, RSSI and SNR, noise floor, TX queue depth, packet counts (flood and direct), duplicates, RX errors, and the repeater's own clock.</li>
        <li><b>Cmd</b> - a command line for raw CLI commands (<code class="inl">ver</code>, <code class="inl">clock</code>, <code class="inl">advert</code>, <code class="inl">get</code> / <code class="inl">set</code>, <code class="inl">neighbors</code>, <code class="inl">reboot</code>...), with the replies logged.</li>
        <li><b>Settings</b> - the remote config: public info, radio, access-control list, regions, neighbours, telemetry, and identity.</li>
      </ul>
      <ShotRow>
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/repeater_cmd.png" alt="repeater command line session" caption="Raw CLI, with replies logged." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/repeater_settings.png" alt="repeater settings tab" caption="Editing the remote config." />
      </ShotRow>
    </section>

    <!-- CLOCK -->
    <section class="sec" id="clock">
      <span class="head">Clock &amp; Airtime</span>
      <h3>Clock</h3>
      <p>Six tabs - Stopwatch, Timer, Pomodoro, Alarm, World clock, and Settings. Time runs in the background, so a stopwatch, timer or Pomodoro block keeps counting after you leave the app, and a timer or Pomodoro phase rings even from another screen.</p>
      <ShotRow>
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/clock_stopwatch.png" alt="stopwatch tab" caption="Stopwatch." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/clock_timer.png" alt="timer tab" caption="Timer." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/clock_alarm.png" alt="alarm tab" caption="Alarm." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/clock_world.png" alt="world clock tab" caption="World clock." />
      </ShotRow>
      <Tip label="Gesture"><p>Long-press <kbd>Select</kbd> to reset the stopwatch, timer or Pomodoro. On the world clock, long-press a city to remove it.</p></Tip>

      <h3>Pomodoro</h3>
      <p>The Pomodoro tab runs the focus-and-break cycle: a ring of segments tracks the set, filling as each focus block completes, with the tomato marking focus and a coffee cup marking the breaks. <b>Select</b> starts and pauses; the phase rings and rolls into the next one on its own.</p>
      <ShotRow>
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/clock_pomodoro.png" alt="pomodoro ready screen" caption="Ready - the empty set ring." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/clock_pomodoro_run.png" alt="pomodoro focus block running" caption="Mid-focus, the ring filling." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/clock_pomodoro_setup.png" alt="pomodoro setup list" caption="Setup: durations and set size." />
      </ShotRow>
      <Tip label="Setup"><p>Long-press <kbd>Select</kbd> on the Ready screen to open setup: focus length, short and long break, how many focus blocks make a set, and whether phases auto-advance or wait for a press.</p></Tip>

      <h3>Airtime</h3>
      <p>Airtime keeps you legal on the band. The Budget tab shows how much of your transmit allowance is free, plus lifetime and last-hour air time. The History tab charts TX and RX over the last hour against a dashed per-minute duty-cycle ceiling.</p>
      <ShotRow>
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/airtime_budget.png" alt="airtime budget gauge" caption="TX headroom at a glance." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/airtime_history.png" alt="airtime history graph" caption="The last hour, minute by minute." />
      </ShotRow>
    </section>

    <!-- SETTINGS -->
    <section class="sec" id="settings">
      <span class="head">Settings</span>
      <p>Grouped by area:</p>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/settings.png" alt="settings groups" caption="Settings, grouped by area." />
      <ul>
        <li><b>Home</b> - screen sleep, the Left / Right home shortcuts, and brightness (previewed live as you step it).</li>
        <li><b>Battery</b> - the indicator style (gauge, percentage, or voltage) and an ADC calibration trim that corrects the reading against a multimeter, with a live voltage preview as you step it.</li>
        <li><b>Contacts</b> - auto-add rules, overwrite-oldest, notify-when-full, max hops, and bulk clean-up.</li>
        <li><b>Messages</b> - auto-retry and auto-path-reset, direct-message acks, per-channel and direct sounds, quick replies.</li>
        <li><b>Advert</b> - your node name and whether adverts share your location.</li>
        <li><b>Radio</b> - preset, frequency, bandwidth, spreading factor, coding rate, TX power, repeater mode. Changes are staged and applied when you leave.</li>
        <li><b>Time &amp; date</b> - timezone by city (DST-aware), 12 / 24h, date format with a live example, auto time-sync, and manual set.</li>
        <li><b>System Info</b> - heap and storage, contact count, battery, uptime, both version numbers, your public key, and two tiers of factory reset.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Most panels drill one level deeper. Battery is a good example: pick how the indicator reads, then step the calibration trim - the modal shows the live voltage next to the percent, so you can dial it against a multimeter.</p>
      <ShotRow>
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/settings_battery.png" alt="battery settings panel" caption="Battery: display style and trim." />
        <Screenshot src="assets/img/settings_battery_cal.png" alt="battery calibration stepper with live voltage" caption="Calibration, with live voltage." />
      </ShotRow>
      <h3>Experimental</h3>
      <p>One drawer for the low-level knobs. Today it holds Path hash size.</p>
      <Tip label="Path hash"><p>Path hash size trades hop capacity for routing precision. <b>1 byte is the safe default</b>; 2 or 3 bytes need repeaters running firmware 1.14 or newer, and each extra byte cuts how many hops a path can hold.</p></Tip>
      <Screenshot src="assets/img/pathhash_row0.png" alt="path hash size setting" caption="The Experimental drawer." />
    </section>
  </article>
</div>
</Base>
